WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.141
What does the boundary measure?

00:00:02.141 --> 00:00:09.374
In Lesson six, we bounded a Legendre coefficient using the largest magnitude of the function on the outer ellipse.

00:00:09.374 --> 00:00:13.684
Now replace that measurement with the root mean square over a full turn.

00:00:13.684 --> 00:00:17.589
Follow the same function, omega squared, through both measurements.

00:00:17.589 --> 00:00:22.383
They lead to different constants and the same geometric decay with degree.

00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:24.435
One angle locates two points.

00:00:24.435 --> 00:00:27.380
Choose the outer circle of radius one and a half.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.663
The cyan point is z on that circle.

00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:35.466
The Joukowski map sends it to the gold point omega on the boundary of the violet ellipse.

00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:38.347
Move the two points by increasing the same angle.

00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:42.252
Equal steps on the circle cover different lengths along the ellipse.

00:00:42.252 --> 00:00:46.555
Each angular step still gets equal weight in the Hardy integral.

00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:48.586
Each height contributes to the mean.

00:00:48.586 --> 00:00:53.749
Raise a cyan stem over the ellipse point by the squared magnitude of omega squared.

00:00:53.749 --> 00:00:58.401
As the point travels, the stem traces a ridge, tallest at the real tips.

00:00:58.401 --> 00:01:03.607
The matching graph unwraps one circuit and shades the accumulated angular energy.

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:08.877
Divide the full area by two pi to get the mean, about zero point five seven nine.

00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:15.783
Its square root, about zero point seven six one, is the Hardy norm of this function on the outer ellipse.

